# The Micro OS SDK @VERSION@

The headers, the library and the link of a program of Micro OS, so that a
program is built **outside the tree of the kernel**: a repository of its own,
a `BUILD` file of a package, a copy on a machine that has no checkout of
Micro OS. This is the SDK of the x86-64 port (the machine of QEMU); the board
is not a target of it (ADR 0018).

The version of the SDK is the version of the kernel it was cut from
(@VERSION@). A program built with it is an image of the `MOX1` format and runs
on a machine of the same release; the format and the numbers of the system
calls change by adding only (ADR 0011).

## What is in it

```text
VERSION                        the kernel version of the SDK
include/                       the headers of a program
  ulibc.h                      the library: every call of it, one page
  stdio.h stdlib.h string.h ...  the POSIX names, each one including ulibc.h
  kernel/ksys.h                the numbers and the structures of the calls
  kernel/exec.h                the header of the MOX1 format
  kernel/errno.h               the errors of the calls
  kernel/types.h vfs.h task.h signal.h   the closure of ksys.h
  prog.h                       the raw door, for a program with no library
lib/libmicroos_user.a          the library of a program, statically linked
lib/crt0.o                     its entry: `_start` of src/start.c
lib/program.ld                 the addresses of the window of the port
bin/mkprog.sh                  the sections of an ELF -> a MOX1 image
bin/link_program.sh            the link of the window, which calls mkprog.sh
bin/microos_sign               the host tool of the signature (ADR 0020)
share/microos-toolchain.cmake  the CMake toolchain of a program
share/build-libc.sh            the recipe that built lib/libmicroos_user.a
share/src/libc/                the sources of that library (MIT, this tree)
share/examples/hello/          a program that builds with this SDK alone
share/README.md                this file
```

There is no `libc.so`, no `ld.so` and no compiler in the SDK: a program is
linked once, statically, at the address of the window of the port, and the
library it names is copied into its image. The C compiler is the one of the
build machine (gcc or clang of x86-64 Linux), used as a freestanding compiler.

## Build a program

With CMake, which is what the toolchain file is for:

```sh
cmake -B build -S share/examples/hello \
    -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=$PWD/share/microos-toolchain.cmake
cmake --build build
file build/hello.mox
```

The image is `build/hello.mox`. It does not need the tree, the kernel's build
or anything of this machine except the SDK and a compiler.

The toolchain sets the system name to `Generic`, takes the headers of the SDK
instead of the ones of the host (`-nostdinc`), compiles with `-Os
-ffreestanding -mno-red-zone -march=x86-64 -fno-pic -fno-pie`, and links the
objects with `lib/crt0.o` and `lib/libmicroos_user.a` through
`bin/link_program.sh` and `lib/program.ld`. `add_executable` is the whole
recipe; there is no `target_link_libraries` to write.

Two options of the toolchain matter:

* `-DMICROOS_FLOAT=ON` is for a program whose own numbers are doubles. The
  port turns the XMM and x87 units on for ring 3 and its scheduler saves
  neither, so one such program runs at a time and `execve` waits for it. A
  program without it is compiled with `-mgeneral-regs-only`, and a `double` in
  its text is a compile error instead of a silent wrong answer.
* `-DMICROOS_GC_SECTIONS=OFF` keeps every function of the archive a program
  names; the default drops what no call reaches.

Without CMake, the three steps are the ones the toolchain runs:

```sh
cc -Os -ffreestanding -nostdinc -isystem $SDK/include \
   -isystem "$(cc -print-file-name=include)" -march=x86-64 -mno-red-zone \
   -fno-pic -fno-pie -fno-stack-protector -ffunction-sections -fdata-sections \
   -c hello.c -o hello.o
sh $SDK/bin/link_program.sh --cc cc --script $SDK/lib/program.ld \
   --entry _start --out hello.mox --base 0x02000000 --arch x86-64 \
   --mkprog $SDK/bin/mkprog.sh -- -Wl,--gc-sections \
   $SDK/lib/crt0.o hello.o $SDK/lib/libmicroos_user.a
```

### The program itself

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char *const argv[], char *const envp[])
{
    printf("hello, %d argument(s)\n", argc);
    return 0;
}
```

`main` is a function of the program: the entry is `_start` of `lib/crt0.o`,
which calls it and then `exit()` with its result (ADR 0016). A `return` from
`_start` would land on nothing. The arguments and the environment are the
vectors the kernel built for the image, as in a Unix-like system.

### The limits of an image

| Limit | Number | Why |
|---|---|---|
| the image | 8 MiB (`K_EXEC_MAX_IMAGE`) | the kernel reads the file into one block of the allocator |
| the bss | 1 MiB (`K_EXEC_BSS_MAX`) | the arena of the library lives in the bss |
| the arena of `malloc` | @HEAP@ bytes | the library was built with `--heap`; see below |
| the window | 16 MiB | from `0x02000000` to `0x03000000`, with a 64 KiB stack at the top |
| one read or write | 64 KiB (`KSYS_MAX_IO`) | the size of one block of the system call |

A program that needs a bigger arena than the one of this SDK rebuilds the
archive with another number and links that instead:

```sh
share/build-libc.sh --src share/src/libc --include include \
    --out lib/libmicroos_user.a --crt0 lib/crt0.o --heap 786432
```

`--no-float` builds the smaller archive of a program that computes in
integers (`-mgeneral-regs-only`, no `%f`, no `strtod` of a double; `sscanf`
still reads the other conversions). The library is built by the same compiler
and cannot be shared
between two programs: each image carries its copy, which is what "statically
linked" means on a machine with no dynamic linker.

### What the machine does not have

* **`mmap`, `brk`, `sbrk`**: the memory of a program is its image, its 64 KiB
  stack and the arena of the library, all decided at link time. There is no
  call to ask the kernel for another page, and `malloc` is a block of the bss
  and not a window of the kernel. A program with a working set bigger than
  the arena does not have a slow path; it has a limit, and the table above
  names it.
* **`execve()` as in Linux**: the call enters the program as a task and
  returns when the program has ended, with its status; the caller waits
  inside the call. A program therefore runs alone while it runs, and a shell
  that starts a program in the background is not a shape of this ABI yet.
* **`fork()` of a kernel task**: `fork()` of a *program* is real on this port
  (the page tables are copied with copy-on-write pages, and the child returns
  0); a task of the kernel has no address space to copy, so a program cannot
  be forked from the shell. The library's `fork()` also works around a debt
  of the port: the page of the stack that holds the return of the call is
  shared until the child runs, so the parent yields once before it uses its
  stack again.
* **`system()`, `popen()`**: there is no `/bin/sh` under a program and no pipe
  from a program; `popen()` answers `ENOSYS`.
* **a terminal**: `isatty()` answers 0, there is no `termios` state beyond the
  one of the console, and the console turns a newline into CR LF by itself.
* **symbolic links**: `symlink()` and `link()` answer `ENOSYS` (the VFS has no
  links); the mode bits and the owner of a file are the ones of the
  filesystem.

The library reports the errors of a system call the way the ABI does: the
negative number of `kernel/errno.h` is also kept in `errno`, so a portable
source reads `-1` and `errno` as it expects.

## The signature, and installing the program

A machine of `1.3` and later installs a program with `pkg`, and `pkg` verifies
the signature of the file (ADR 0024). The SDK carries the tool of the
signature:

```sh
bin/microos_sign keygen --key my.key --pub my.pub
bin/microos_sign sign-file --key my.key --in hello.mox --sig hello.mox.sig
```

A `.mox` with no signature is a file a person may put on a disk they wrote
themselves; an install from a repository is a signed file and an index, and a
copyleft package also carries its license and its source (ADR 0025).

The image of a disk of the machine is written by the tree
(`tools/x86_image.py --program /bin/hello=hello.mox`); the SDK does not write
one.

## License

The files of this SDK are the sources of Micro OS and are MIT (the `LICENSE`
of the tree). The library and the headers are copied from the tree at the
version above; a program built with them is the program of its own author and
carries the license of that author.
